# November 21, 2026: Glens Falls, with Queensbury and Hudson Falls (Kingsbury). Drawn at random by
# tools/choose.py --state ny (region wash, which offered East Whitehall); start.py showed the Atlas had no place in the
# city of Glens Falls itself (22 Register listings there), only the Feeder Canal ruins east of it.
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py); HMdb marker pages (tools/page.py);
# GlensFalls.com's "History of Iconic Glens Falls Buildings" pages; historypointer.com for Register street addresses;
# the New York State Military Museum's armory page; the Preservation League of New York State and the Glens Falls
# Chronicle on the Ferguson office; the Glens Falls Business Journal on the old Washington County courthouse; the
# Hyde Collection's, Crandall Public Library's and First Presbyterian Church's own pages for hours.
# NPGallery forms for Warren County listings before 2010 are the one-page placeholder and were not tried again.
# Red brick: from the Commons photographs each place's Wikipedia article names, opened with tools/brick.py look
# (the armory from its 2015 photograph, not the 1907 postcard); '?' where no picture of the place itself was opened.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'wash_nthw','label':'Glens Falls, with Queensbury and Hudson Falls','added':'2026-11-21 04:44'}
GFB=lambda slug,t: L('GlensFalls.com: '+t,'https://www.glensfalls.com/history/buildings/'+slug+'/')
HM=lambda n,t: L('HMdb: '+t,'https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m='+n)
HP=lambda ref,t: L('historypointer: '+t+' (Register address)','https://historypointer.com/nris/resource/'+ref)
CITY=' On-street parking and city lots downtown; which lots are public and their rules were not checked.'
PLACES=[
# ---- Glens Falls: museums and the library ----
N('hyde-collection-glens-falls','The Hyde Collection (Hyde House) and the Pruyn sisters\' houses','Glens Falls','wash','landmark','standing',43.31023,-73.63411,'exact','open','drive','','1904–1912',
  'Samuel Pruyn, a founder of Finch, Pruyn & Co., had three daughters, and between 1904 and 1912 each built a house on adjoining lots on Warren Street above the Hudson, all designed by the Boston architect Henry Forbes Bigelow: Hoopes House (1904, Dutch Colonial Revival), Cunningham House (1910-11) and, between them, Hyde House (finished 1912, Renaissance Revival) for Charlotte Pruyn Hyde and her husband Louis. The Hydes filled it with Old Master and American paintings; in 1952 Charlotte set up a trust that made the house and collection a public art museum. A 1989 wing by Edward Larrabee Barnes joined Hyde House to Cunningham House.',
  'Hyde House itself, shown as the Hydes furnished it: Wikipedia lists works by Botticelli, El Greco, Rembrandt, Rubens, Degas, Picasso, Homer and Whistler among the collection. The museum\'s page says two rooms of the house (the downstairs guest bedroom and Mrs. Hyde\'s bedroom) cannot be reached by wheelchair, and that the courtyard is rented for weddings. Hoopes House stands next door at 153 Warren Street. A 2010s Commons photograph shows the house\'s walls grey (stucco or stone), not brick.',
  'Old Masters in a paper-mill heiress\'s house.',
  'A museum run by The Hyde Collection trust, at 161 Warren Street. Its page gives general admission $12, seniors (60+) $10, free to all on the second Thursday of each month, and closed on Thanksgiving, Christmas, New Year\'s Day and Independence Day. Parking not checked.',
  [W('The Hyde Collection'),L('The Hyde Collection: hours and admission','https://www.hydecollection.org/hours-admission/'),
   L('The Hyde Collection: museum facts','https://www.hydecollection.org/museum-facts/'),GFB('hyde-house','Pruyn sisters\' homes and the Hyde Collection')],
  brick='no',hours='Thursday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('chapman-museum-glens-falls','Chapman Museum (Zopher DeLong House)','Glens Falls','wash','landmark','standing',43.31152,-73.64901,'exact','open','drive','','early 1800s; remodelled 1867',
  'Zopher and Catherine DeLong, a hardware merchant\'s family, bought a plain early 19th-century wooden house on Glen Street in 1860. After the 1864 fire burned downtown, they rebuilt it in brick as a two-storey Second Empire house with a mansard roof (Wikipedia gives 1867, the Register about 1870). Their son John modernised it after Zopher died in 1901. Juliet Chapman, a relative by marriage, gave it to the community about sixty years later, and it is the local history museum of the Glens Falls-Queensbury Historical Association.',
  'The brick house with its mansard roof, central two-storey pavilion and bracketed entrance porch; the HMdb marker notes the cast-iron window mouldings and brackets. The original carriage house is on the lot. Inside, the museum keeps the house as a period home beside its exhibits (Wikipedia); rooms not checked.',
  'A hardware merchant\'s house rebuilt in brick after the fire of 1864.',
  'Owned and run by the Glens Falls-Queensbury Historical Association, on Glen Street. Its own site and the Town of Queensbury\'s page both refused the page reader; hours not checked.'+CITY,
  [W('Chapman Museum'),W('Zopher Delong House'),HM('147865','The DeLong House')],brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('crandall-library-glens-falls','Crandall Public Library','Glens Falls','wash','civic','standing',43.31091,-73.64556,'exact','open','drive','','1931',
  'Henry Crandall, who made a fortune in lumber and real estate, offered in 1892 to pay for a free library; it opened in one of his Bay Street business blocks in 1893. When those blocks were razed in 1919 for City Park, the library moved into the Jerome Lapham house on Ridge Street, and in 1931 it opened its own building, designed by Charles Platt of New York. Additions followed in 1969 and, in 2008, a large wing by Ann Beha Architects.',
  'Platt\'s 1931 building facing City Park, with what GlensFalls.com calls the wood-panelled reading room of the 1930s, joined to the 2008 glass wing. The Folklife Center on the lower level and the research room, which keeps a leaf of a Gutenberg Bible and Look magazine\'s "Hometown USA" photographs of Glens Falls.',
  'A lumberman\'s free library, still the city\'s.',
  'A public library at 251 Glen Street; hours from the library\'s own page (whether the Sunday hours run all year was not checked).'+CITY,
  [GFB('crandall-library','Crandall Public Library'),HM('172478','History of Crandall Public Library'),L('Crandall Public Library','https://crandalllibrary.org/')],
  brick='?',hours='Monday to Thursday 9 a.m. to 9 p.m.; Friday and Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; Sunday 1 to 5 p.m.'),
# ---- downtown ----
N('three-squares-glens-falls','Three Squares: downtown Glens Falls, City Hall and the Empire Theatre','Glens Falls','wash','town','standing',43.31145,-73.64387,'area','public','walk','','1864–1930',
  'The commercial centre of Glens Falls, listed as the Three Squares Historic District in 1984 (75 contributing buildings; boundary changed in 2022). Fires in 1862 (Wikipedia) or 1864 (GlensFalls.com), 1884 and 1902 burned much of it, so most buildings date from 1902 to 1930. City Park was a city block of houses and shops until Henry Crandall began clearing it in 1907; the last house went in 1931.',
  'Round City Park: the Neoclassical City Hall (1900, Fuller & Pitcher of Albany) at 42 Ridge Street, the Queensbury Hotel and Crandall Library. On South Street, the Empire Theatre (1898-99), granite with a terra-cotta front, which closed as a theatre in the 1940s and since 2008 holds shops, offices and apartments. On Glen Street, the Beaux-Arts First National Bank (1914-15, granite and Vermont marble, bronze doors), a TD Bank branch until 2015, and the Church of the Messiah. At 6 Warren Street, the D.H. Cowles building, designed by Marcus F. Cummings after the 1864 fire. On Warren Street, the former post office (1912-15). Interiors not checked.',
  'Three fires and the city that was rebuilt after them.',
  'Public streets and City Park; the buildings are offices, shops and homes, seen from the street.'+CITY,
  [W('Three Squares Historic District'),GFB('city-hall','City Hall'),GFB('empire-theatre','The Empire Theatre'),GFB('first-national-bank','First National Bank'),
   GFB('post-office','Former post office'),HM('138815','City Park\'s Development')],brick='?'),
N('queensbury-hotel-glens-falls','The Queensbury Hotel','Glens Falls','wash','hotel','standing',43.31181,-73.64416,'exact','open','drive','','1924–26',
  'Glens Falls businessmen wanted a grand hotel from 1916, for travellers bound for the Adirondacks and as a meeting place. On March 6, 1924, a hundred of them met at the Glens Falls Insurance Company and formed a hotel corporation; Glens Falls Insurance and Finch, Pruyn each took $50,000 of stock and $440,000 was raised in eight days. The hotel opened on May 7, 1926, at a cost of about $760,000 (GlensFalls.com; Wikipedia says built in 1926). It added 56 rooms in 1929 and a cocktail lounge in 1934, and is still a working hotel.',
  'The Colonial Revival hotel on Ridge Street facing City Park and City Hall. Wikipedia mentions mahogany in the interior and on the room doors; after renovations it has 125 rooms, the Queen\'s Ballroom and Fenimore\'s Pub. GlensFalls.com lists guests from Louis Armstrong to Robert F. Kennedy, who came back the day after winning his 1964 Senate race. Lobby not checked.',
  'The town-built hotel that every Adirondack traveller passed through.',
  'A working hotel on Ridge Street; the public rooms are for guests and diners. The street address used for the pin (88 Ridge Street) was matched by the geocoder, not read on the hotel\'s page; hours not checked.',
  [W('Queensbury Hotel'),GFB('queensbury-hotel','The Queensbury Hotel')],brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('ferguson-office-glens-falls','Dr. James Ferguson Office','Glens Falls','wash','landmark','standing',43.31067,-73.63890,'exact','view','drive','','about 1870',
  'A tiny Second Empire doctor\'s office of about 1870, with a slate mansard roof and one centre dormer; its Register form calls it the only building of its type and style left in the city. Dr. James Ferguson, the Chronicle says, later bought the top of Prospect Mountain and built a hotel and a sanatorium there. The city took the building for unpaid taxes in 2014 and sought demolition bids in 2017, with a hole open in the north wall. Darren and Lisa Tracy bought it for $1, saved the brick walls and the mansard and made it apartments; the Preservation League gave it an award in 2020.',
  'The little mansard-roofed building at 5 Culvert Street, just off Warren Street. Rental apartments inside: look from the street.',
  'Saved for a dollar the week it was to be pulled down.',
  'Private apartments; view from Culvert Street.'+CITY,
  [W('Dr. James Ferguson Office'),HM('148326','The onetime Dr. James Ferguson Office'),
   L('Preservation League of NYS: Excellence Award spotlight','https://preservenys.org/blog/excellence-award-spotlight-dr-fergusons-office'),
   L('Glens Falls Chronicle: Save this house! (2017)','https://www.glensfallschronicle.com/save-this-house-5-culvert-street-glens-falls/')],brick='yes'),
N('glens-falls-armory','18th Separate Company Armory','Glens Falls','wash','civic','standing',43.31020,-73.63592,'exact','view','drive','','1895',
  'A castle-like National Guard armory of brick and stone, designed by the State Architect Isaac G. Perry, who finished the State Capitol, and first occupied on November 11, 1895. Its first unit was the 18th Separate Company, organised in 1876, which served in the Spanish-American War; later units went from here to the Mexican border and both World Wars, and a medical company used it into the 1990s. The New York State Military Museum gives its status as closed.',
  'The 65-foot corner tower with its battlemented parapet, narrow recessed windows and hipped slate roofs; the museum\'s page adds the timber-built drill hall behind and a 1959 vehicle garage. Interior not checked; the building is closed.',
  'Isaac Perry\'s castle on Warren Street.',
  'At 147 Warren Street. Closed (state military museum page); who owns it now was not checked. View from the street.'+CITY,
  [W('18th Separate Company Armory'),L('NYS Military Museum: Glens Falls Armory','https://museum.dmna.ny.gov/armories-arsenals/glens-falls-armory'),HM('148660','18th Separate Company Armory')],brick='yes'),
N('shirt-factory-glens-falls','The Shirt Factory (McMullen-Leavens shirt factory)','Glens Falls','wash','industry','standing',43.31347,-73.63668,'exact','open','drive','','1902–1993',
  'After the fire of 1902 burned the Fowler shirt and collar works on Glen Street, J.R. McMullen and Walter Leavens built a new shirt factory by the railroad depot on Lawrence Street, designed by Ephraim B. Potter as a three-storey block and a rear laundry; by 1910 it covered 60,000 square feet. From 1936 it also made dresses, with over 800 women working for it, half at home. Production was moved away in stages from 1956, and the Tom James Co. closed it in the 1990s. Eric Unkauf bought it in 1999 and filled it with artists\' studios, shops and offices.',
  'The L-shaped brick factory between Lawrence and Cooper streets, now The Shirt Factory Arts & Health Center: GlensFalls.com counts nearly a hundred tenants, galleries and classes, and a summer market and food trucks on Thursdays. Original machinery was auctioned in 1996.',
  'A shirt and dress factory turned over to artists.',
  'Privately owned, at 71 Lawrence Street / 21 Cooper Street; studios and shops open to the public. No opening hours were found on its home page.'+CITY,
  [GFB('shirt-factory','The Shirt Factory'),L('The Shirt Factory','https://shirtfactorygf.com/')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('peyser-morrison-shirt-glens-falls','Peyser and Morrison Shirt Company Building','Glens Falls','wash','industry','standing',43.31011,-73.62964,'site','view','drive','','1893',
  'A square two-storey brick factory of 1893 with an elaborate Romanesque street front. Wikipedia calls it the only industrial building left by the Glens Falls architect Ephraim B. Potter (1855-1925); it was listed with his other buildings in 1984. (GlensFalls.com credits him with the Lawrence Street shirt factory of 1902 as well, so the two sources disagree.)',
  'The decorated street front at 211-217 Warren Street. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private; view from Warren Street.'+CITY,
  [W('Peyser and Morrison Shirt Company Building'),HP('84003388','Peyser and Morrison Shirt Company Building')],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('bemis-eye-sanitarium-glens-falls','Bemis Eye Sanitarium and the Sherman House','Glens Falls','wash','inst','standing',43.31222,-73.65111,'site','view','drive','','1840s–1902',
  'Dr. Edward Herbert Bemis built a sanitarium for eye diseases here between about 1893 and 1902: five boarding houses designed by Ephraim Potter, plus two older houses and a carriage house turned to its use. It closed after he died in 1901 (Wikipedia gives both that year and the 1902 date for the buildings). One of the older houses is the Sherman House, a pink-brick house thought to date from the 1840s, with a heavily bracketed roof and an octagonal cupola; its front veranda and rear wing were added about 1900 for the sanitarium. Two buildings on Sherman Avenue, made into the Sherman Square Apartments, were gutted by fire on May 8, 2014, with the outer walls kept.',
  'The Sherman House with its cupola, and the Queen Anne boarding houses round Glen Street and Sherman Avenue. Whether the burned apartments were rebuilt was not checked. All private homes: look from the street.',
  'A doctor\'s eye-cure village of boarding houses.',
  'Private houses and apartments; view from Glen Street and Sherman Avenue.'+CITY,
  [W('Bemis Eye Sanitarium Complex'),W('Sherman House (Glens Falls, New York)')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
# ---- churches ----
N('first-presbyterian-glens-falls','First Presbyterian Church of Glens Falls','Glens Falls','wash','worship','standing',43.31266,-73.65074,'exact','open','drive','','1926–1929',
  'A large stone Gothic church in a cross plan by Ralph Adams Cram, the leading church architect of his day. It took three years to build; the marker on it gives the completion date as June 9, 1929.',
  'The stone church at the corner of Glen Street and West Notre Dame Street. Interior and glass not checked.',
  'A Ralph Adams Cram church in a mill town.',
  'At 400 Glen Street; an active church. Services and office hours from its own page, which asks visitors outside service times to call or write first.'+CITY,
  [W('First Presbyterian Church (Glens Falls, New York)'),HM('148204','First Presbyterian Church of Glens Falls'),L('First Presbyterian Church of Glens Falls','https://www.fpcgf.org/')],
  brick='no',hours='Sunday worship 10 a.m.; office Monday to Friday 9 a.m. to 2 p.m.'),
N('church-of-the-messiah-glens-falls','Church of the Messiah (Episcopal)','Glens Falls','wash','worship','standing',43.31090,-73.64755,'exact','view','drive','','1854–1865',
  'Episcopalians in Glens Falls organised in 1840 and met in homes, then in a wooden chapel on Ridge Street from 1843. This stone Gothic Revival church, designed by John W. Summers, took eleven years to build; GlensFalls.com says it was one of the few buildings to come through the fire of 1864, was finished for Christmas 1865, and was consecrated in June 1866, which the Episcopal Diocese of Albany confirms.',
  'The towered stone church at 296 Glen Street, inside the Three Squares district. Interior not checked.',
  'The church that outlasted the fire of 1864.',
  'An active parish of the Episcopal Diocese of Albany. No service times were read on a page that could be shown to be this church\'s, so none are given.'+CITY,
  [GFB('church-of-the-messiah','Church of the Messiah'),HM('147758','The Church of the Messiah (Episcopal)'),
   L('Episcopal Diocese of Albany: Church of the Messiah','https://albanyepiscopaldiocese.org/church-of-the-messiah-glens-falls-ny/')],brick='no'),
N('society-of-friends-hall-glens-falls','Society of Friends Hall','Glens Falls','wash','worship','standing',43.31643,-73.64401,'exact','view','drive','','1875',
  'A Quaker meeting house of 1875, attributed to the architect Marcus F. Cummings of Troy: a two-storey Italianate building of brick with tall round-arched windows and small buttressed corner towers. Quakers were Queensbury\'s first settlers (see the burying ground on Quaker Road).',
  'The arched windows and corner towers at 172-174 Ridge Street. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'View from Ridge Street; who uses the building now was not checked.'+CITY,
  [W('Society of Friends Hall'),HP('84003407','Society of Friends Hall')],brick='yes'),
N('glens-falls-cemetery','Glens Falls Cemetery','Glens Falls','wash','burial','standing',43.3211,-73.6536,'site','public','drive','cemeteries','1853',
  'The village bought 13 acres from Andrew Porteus of Queensbury for $1,000 in 1853; the first burial was in 1855, and older stones were moved here from the West Street burying ground in the early 1870s. It grew by stages to about 32 to 38 acres (Wikipedia gives both) and holds over 12,000 burials.',
  'The Memorial Chapel of 1946, a small stone building with a slate roof; the 1950 office at Hope Avenue and Ogden Street; mausoleums and monuments, and the stones brought from West Street.',
  '',
  'Bought by the village in 1853 (Wikipedia); who runs it now, its rules and gate hours were not checked, so keep to daylight and the drives. Enter from Hope Avenue / Ogden Street, where the office is.',
  [W('Glens Falls Cemetery')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
# ---- Queensbury ----
N('queensbury-quaker-burying-ground','Queensbury Quaker Burying Ground','Queensbury','wash','burial','marker',43.33194,-73.65306,'site','public','drive','cemeteries','about 1765–1837',
  'The first burying ground in Queensbury, used by the Friends\' meeting from about 1765 to 1837; a meeting house and school stood here too. About 80 people lie here, with no gravestones, as was Quaker custom. The Wing family put up a memorial in 1911 to Abraham Wing, the town\'s founder. An archaeological survey before a shopping centre was built in 2002 found the burials, and the land was deeded to the town.',
  'A fenced patch of grass beside a shopping centre with two plaques, the 1911 Wing memorial and a 1999 state marker. Nothing else stands.',
  'Queensbury\'s first settlers, under a lawn by a shopping centre.',
  'Town of Queensbury land at the southwest corner of Quaker Road (Route 254) and Bay Road. The nearest lot belongs to the shopping centre.',
  [W('Queensbury Quaker Burying Ground'),HM('18106','Old Quaker Burying Ground'),HM('18226','Wing Memorial')],brick='no'),
N('sanford-stower-houses-queensbury','Sanford House and Asa Stower House, Ridge Road','Queensbury','wash','landmark','standing',43.35167,-73.61985,'exact','view','drive','','about 1797–1806',
  'Two Federal houses on Ridge Road, both on the Register. The Sanford House (749 Ridge Road), of about 1797, is a formal five-bay, two-storey frame house with Georgian touches, wide clapboards, an elaborate centre porch and a Palladian window above it. The Asa Stower House (693 Ridge Road) grew in four stages: a frame house from before 1806 now in the rear ell, the main block of about 1806, Greek Revival changes about 1850 and Italianate porches about 1870.',
  'The Sanford House\'s porch and Palladian window, and about 400 m south the Stower House with its slate roof. Private homes: look from Ridge Road (Route 9L).',
  'Two of the oldest houses in the Glens Falls country.',
  'Private homes; view from Ridge Road only.',
  [W('Sanford House (Queensbury, New York)'),W('Asa Stower House'),HP('98000549','Sanford House'),HP('06000261','Asa Stower House')],brick='no'),
# ---- Hudson Falls (Kingsbury) ----
N('hudson-falls-village-park-courthouse','Hudson Falls village park and the old Washington County Courthouse','Hudson Falls','wash','town','standing',43.30054,-73.58555,'site','public','walk','','about 1810–1935',
  'The centre of the old village of Sandy Hill, listed as the Hudson Falls Historic District in 1983: 148 contributing buildings round the village park, most of them built between 1875 and 1900. Washington County was a shire county with two court houses, at Sandy Hill and Salem; the first here was built in 1806, and the present one, designed by Marcus Cummings of Troy, in 1873. It served until 1993, when the county seat moved to Fort Edward. Among the oldest buildings is the law office of about 1810 at 177 Main Street, where Henry C. Martindale worked with his clerk, the later governor Silas Wright.',
  'The 1873 courthouse at 122 Main Street, the park with its fountain and Civil War monument, the little law office, the separately listed post office and the houses round them, among them Springside (about 1815). The Glens Falls Business Journal reported in 2017 that the group Phoenix Rising Hudson Falls owned the courthouse and was renovating it, with a restaurant opening inside; its use today was not checked.',
  'A shire town\'s court square, kept whole.',
  'Public streets, and the park the district is centred on (who owns the park was not checked). A 1999 Town of Kingsbury marker stands at Main and Maple streets. Street parking; lots not checked.',
  [W('Hudson Falls Historic District'),HM('303529','Washington County Court House'),
   L('Glens Falls Business Journal: restaurant in the old courthouse (2017)','https://www.glensfalls.com/glensfallsbusinessjournal/2017/05/new-restaurant-hudson-falls-forged-open-old-washington-county-courthouse')],brick='?'),
N('moss-street-school-kingsbury','Moss Street School','Hudson Falls','wash','civic','standing',43.32038,-73.57685,'exact','view','drive','','1884',
  'Kingsbury\'s District 4 school appears on the 1853 county map; the town bought the lot from Thomas and Prudence Daily for $18 in 1849, and the present building is recorded as built in 1884. It joined the Hudson Falls central schools in the 1950s, then served as a Seventh-day Adventist school, and is now the Washington County Head Start school. The 2007 town marker says no school building in the county has been used as a school longer without a break.',
  'The 1884 schoolhouse at Dix Avenue (Route 32) and Vaughn Road, with the marker on the front lawn.',
  '',
  'A working Head Start school: look from Dix Avenue and do not go onto the grounds in school hours.',
  [HM('262158','Moss Street School')],brick='?'),
]
